{"title":"What is Quarto?","markdown":{"headingText":"What is Quarto?","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nQuarto is a modern publishing system that allows you to create teaching materials from simple plain-text source files and publish them to a range of formats including:\n\n- HTML websites\n- PDF documents\n- Microsoft Word documents\n- PowerPoint presentations\n- Reveal.js slide decks\n- Books and collections of lecture notes\n\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n\nThis guide introduces the fundamentals of Quarto using a **Visual Studio Code (VS Code) workflow.**\n\nAlternatively you can also use Quarto through RStudio, the command line and other routes too!\n\n:::\n\n\n## Why Use Quarto?\n\n### Write Once, Publish Anywhere\n\nA single source document can be rendered as:\n\n- A website for online reading\n- A printable PDF\n- A Word document\n- Presentation slides\n\n### Accessible formats\n\nHTML output can provide:\n\n- Responsive design\n- Screen reader support\n- Improved navigation\n- Adjustable text size\n- Better support for mobile devices (compared to PDFs)\n\n### Plain Text Source Files\n\nQuarto files are plain text, making them:\n\n- Easy to edit\n- Easy to archive\n- Suitable for version control systems such as Git\n- Portable across operating systems\n\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n\nIf you're from a LaTeX background or bookdown then you'll find the equation syntax and referencing familiar (citations can be managed with a .bib file). \n\nHowever, given how extensible LaTeX is, you won't have access to all the packages you may be familiar with.\n\n:::\n\n\n### Rich content\nYou can:\n\n  -   embed content (e.g. video, interactive widgets)\n  -   execute code and render outputs (e.g. Python, R)\n\n\n## The anatomy of a Quarto project\n\nA typical Quarto project consists of:\n\n- A project folder\n- A `_quarto.yml` configuration file that sets project-level settings (e.g. formatting options, output formats). Similar to a LaTeX preamble, it's useful to have this separately from the source files so you can easily re-use it.\n- One or more Quarto source files (with the extension `.qmd`). This is written in markdown and is the main bulk of your document.\n- a folder for images and other resources\n\nOptionally, you may also include:\n- a bibliography file (with the extension `.bib`) for citations\n- a folder for custom scripts (e.g. Python, R) that can be executed during the rendering process\n- a folder for custom extensions (e.g. for new output formats or features - see [Quarto extensions](https://quarto.org/docs/extensions/))\n\n## The Quarto stack\n\nQuarto helps automate the process of converting your source content and configuration into a range of publication formats. You write your content in a `.qmd` file and control how it is rendered using YAML settings (either within the document or in project-level `.yml` files). Quarto then takes care of the remaining steps, so you can focus on writing content rather than managing complex publishing workflows.\n\n**Rendering workflow**\n\n1. **You write content** in a `.qmd` file using Markdown, equations, images, and optional code (e.g. additional .py, .R, .ipynb files).\n2. **You define settings** using YAML metadata (your `_quarto.yml` file or a YAML block within the document).\n3. **Quarto executes code** (if present) using **Knitr, Jupyter**, or another supported engine.\n4. **Quarto converts content** using Pandoc.\n5. **Quarto generates final output** in one or more formats. This includes HTML books (*like this one*), PDF documents (via LaTeX) and other formats (e.g. Word, Reveal.js, EPUB)\n\nIn practice, most authors only need to maintain their `.qmd` files and YAML settings (steps 1 and 2 above). Quarto automates the execution, conversion, and creation steps, allowing you to create professional outputs from a single source document.","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7